Original commit body from 13a5966abf5a7180c3301d16274f352185302a8b:

WI-gihos was filed to decide what a wrapper like BufReader inherits from the
reader it wraps. Enumerating std::io in total -- 556 items across 15 files of
the installed rustc 1.94.0 library/std/src -- answered a structural question
first: WHERE MAY A ROW BE KEYED AT ALL. A row's module must be a type that
receiver inference can produce, and three kinds cannot be.

A TRAIT cannot. No rust value has type `Read`; values have type File,
TcpStream, BufReader<R>. gate_named_entry drops method-kind hits when there is
no module hint, and a trait can never BE that hint. Measured across the 42-repo
cohort: every edge whose slot is std::io::Read / ::Write / ::BufRead carries
the name `module` -- a `use` import, never a call.

A WRAPPER cannot, and this is the item's own question answered by measurement
rather than by taste. A wrapper's boundary is the boundary of what it wraps,
which the catalogue cannot know. In `rage`, 8 of ~10 BufReader::new sites wrap
an IN-MEMORY BYTE SLICE, so an fs_read row would be majority-wrong on the first
real repository to exercise it. This lands OPPOSITE to refuse-by-default
intuition, which would have added it.

A GENERIC HELPER cannot. io::copy's measured call site is
`fn write_output<R: io::Read, W: io::Write>(input: R, output: W)` -- generic by
construction, so its boundary is unknowable rather than merely unknown.

So the trait methods go on the CONCRETE TYPES. Not a new invention: it is what
std::net::TcpStream already did, and what python.yaml does with WI-fuvuj's
synthetic `file` module rather than putting read/write on io.IOBase.
std::fs::File was the inconsistency -- it implements Read, Write and Seek and
carried NONE of their methods while TcpStream carried its equivalents.

49 rows added: File's read and write families; the four concrete stdio handles
and their Lock variants (Stdin/StdinLock ipc_recv, Stdout/Stderr and locks
logging), because .lock() moves no data and without them a read through
stdin().lock() is the same gap one level down; and PipeReader/PipeWriter
(ipc_recv/ipc_send) while pipe() itself stays absent as descriptor plumbing.

THE ROWS ARE CORRECT AND INERT, AND BOTH HALVES ARE STATED HERE RATHER THAN
FOUND LATER. Measured across 11,691 rust call edges in 42 repositories, the
rust catalogue matches ZERO method-kind primitives -- every match is
function-kind. The 78 genuine trait-method call edges (46 write_all, 15
read_to_end, 10 read_to_string, 6 read_line) all arrive as
rust:external:0-0:<name>:external_symbol with a null dst_ref, which
gate_named_entry rightly refuses. That is INV-linub L3/L4, and it makes EVERY
method row in rust.yaml dead today, TcpStream's included: a file read and a
socket read are equally invisible. Adding correct rows does not change that and
is not claimed to. Measured both ways -- starvation delta across 42 surveys
frees nothing and strands nothing; classification delta reclassifies 0 edges.
What this buys is a catalogue that is true and self-consistent, live the moment
receiver evidence is. The rust arm of INV-linub's exposure table is recorded on
that item at findings level rather than edge level.

Every refusal is pinned as a test, not as prose: the traits, the wrappers,
copy/read_to_string, empty/repeat/sink, pipe, .lock(), Error/ErrorKind (70 of
the 102 measured bare-std::io edges are error handling and none of it is I/O),
seek/rewind/stream_position (a syscall, but nothing crosses -- the try_clone
rule), flush (python.yaml excludes it deliberately: it commits writes already
counted), the read adapters, and everything unstable in rustc 1.94.0.

is_terminal is genuinely borderline and is recorded as such: isatty(2) moves no
data, but python.yaml rows os.isatty as fs_read and points the other way. Both
sides are written into the catalogue header so it can be overturned without
re-deriving it.

.ci/affected-tests.txt is extended by hand with the 20 catalogue-exercising
test files (INV-bigaz: the slicer has no YAML->Python edge, so a catalogue
change selects ~1 file). Full suite green: 24,095 passed, 100% coverage.

